
RewriteEngine on

# ------------------------------- RULE: canonical host (must come FIRST)
#
# Search engines treat these as four separate websites serving identical pages:
#
#     http://nolimitmedia.ca/…        https://nolimitmedia.ca/…
#     http://www.nolimitmedia.ca/…    https://www.nolimitmedia.ca/…
#
# That splits ranking signals across hostnames and can produce duplicate entries
# in Search Console / Bing Webmaster Tools. These two rules fold every variant
# onto ONE address — https://nolimitmedia.ca — with a permanent (301) redirect.
#
# Both rules are keyed on the real production hostname, so they never fire under
# MAMP (localhost / 127.0.0.1) and local development is unaffected.
#
# The X-Forwarded-Proto check matters on shared hosting: when TLS is terminated
# by a proxy, %{HTTPS} is not "on" even though the visitor is already on https,
# and without it the rule would redirect to itself in a loop.

# www  ->  apex (and straight to https in the same hop)
#
# Loop-safe by construction: after the redirect the host is the apex form, so
# this condition can never match again.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.nolimitmedia\.ca$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^ https://nolimitmedia.ca%{REQUEST_URI} [L,R=301]

# NOTE — plain http:// is deliberately NOT handled here.
# Pages already 301 to https via the framework; only static files served
# directly by Apache (sitemap.xml, robots.txt) are reachable over http, which
# is a negligible SEO issue. An .htaccess http->https rule is the one rule that
# can cause an infinite redirect loop if the host terminates TLS at a proxy and
# exposes neither %{HTTPS} nor X-Forwarded-Proto. If we want it, the safe route
# is cPanel's built-in "Force HTTPS Redirect" toggle, which the host configures
# correctly for its own infrastructure.

# ------------------------------- HOW DGZ ROUTING WORKS
#
# This file's only job is to decide whether a request should be handled by
# Apache directly (real files/directories) or handed to the DGZ framework
# (everything else).
#
# When a request reaches Apache:
#   1. If the path maps to a real file on disk (CSS, JS, images, etc.)
#      → Apache serves it directly. PHP never runs.
#   2. If the path maps to a real directory on disk
#      → Apache serves it directly. PHP never runs.
#   3. Everything else
#      → Forwarded to index.php. DGZ_Router takes over from there.
#
# DGZ_Router reads the full REQUEST_URI and handles all routing logic:
#   - Defined routes (routes/web.php, routes/api.php) are checked first.
#   - Automatic route discovery (controller/method/id convention) is the fallback.
#
# Because ALL routing decisions are made inside PHP, this file does not need
# to know anything about URL depth or segment counts. A single catch-all rule
# covers every possible URL — 1 segment or 10.

# ------------------------------- RULE: empty path → home
RewriteRule ^$ index.php [L]

# ------------------------------- RULE: catch-all (any depth)
# Only fires when the path is NOT a real file and NOT a real directory.
# The [L] flag stops processing further rules once this one matches.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
RewriteRule ^ index.php [L]

# ------------------------------- PHP upload limits
#increase the PHP max file upload size and max post size to enable the uploading of bigger files
<IfModule mod_php7.c>
   php_value upload_max_filesize 50M
   php_value post_max_size 50M
   php_value memory_limit 256M
</IfModule>
<IfModule mod_php8.c>
   php_value upload_max_filesize 50M
   php_value post_max_size 50M
   php_value memory_limit 256M
</IfModule>
